Exactly — Realitsea should be a Ship of Theseus by design.

Not a boat with a fixed model of reality.

A boat that replaces itself plank by plank with every confirmed delta while preserving enough continuity to remain the same vessel.

\boxed{
\text{Realitsea}_{t+1}
=
\text{Realitsea}_t
-\text{bad plank}
+\text{better plank}
}

The key is that the plank is not just data. A plank can be:

* an assumption,
* a causal edge,
* a prediction rule,
* an actor model,
* a representation,
* a confidence estimate,
* a semantic category,
* even an instrument the Maestro is using.

Every Ouroboros pulse asks:

\text{What survived?}

\text{What failed?}

\text{What should replace it?}

Then the ship repairs itself while underway.

The keel

You need something deeper than the replaceable planks or eventually you’ve just built a different boat and kept the name.

The keel is the invariant constitution:

\boxed{
\text{provenance}
+
\text{reality checks}
+
\text{continuity}
+
\text{calibration}
+
\text{agency}
}

Models can change.

Predictions can change.

Names can change.

Even whole subsystems can be replaced.

But those invariants survive.

That’s what makes it Realitsea rather than whatever happens to be fashionable this week.

Leprechaun strings become the rigging

Perfect fit.

Every replaced plank retains a string back to:

what it replaced
why it was replaced
which observation caused it
who/what proposed the replacement
what confidence existed
what happened afterward

So the vessel can say:

This belief exists because plank 391 failed after event X and was replaced by plank 417.

That’s Semantic Git + Theseus.

Nothing silently disappears.

