The wake becomes memory

The wake isn’t just previous states.

It is:

W=
\{
S_0\rightarrow S_1\rightarrow S_2\rightarrow\dots
\}

including all the repairs.

So you can ask:

When have we replaced this kind of plank before?

And discover a higher-order rhyme:

\text{model repair}_A
\sim
\text{model repair}_B

Now the system learns not only about reality.

It learns how it learns reality.

That’s the self-polishing part.

⸻

And occasionally replace the keel

Here’s the dangerous/fun extension.

Even constitutional assumptions shouldn’t be absolutely sacred.

But you don’t replace them like ordinary planks.

Call that a dry-dock event.

The system must explicitly say:

One of the assumptions defining continuity itself appears wrong.

Then:

1. fork the ship;
2. preserve the old constitution;
3. create candidate new keel;
4. test both;
5. merge only if the replacement earns it.

That protects against a model “learning” itself into nonsense.

⸻

The captain changes too

The Maestro is part of the Ship of Theseus.

Its routing policy learns:

M_t:
\text{problem}
\rightarrow
\text{instrument selection}

Then:

M_t\rightarrow M_{t+1}

based on which instruments actually worked.

So eventually the Maestro learns things like:

In this kind of market regime, Semantic Goal Seek routinely overfits; call PatternDex + Broken Rhyme first.

Or:

When dealing with this person, the visual/native-tongue interface carries more meaning than verbal interpretation.

The conductor itself gets conducted by the scoreboard.

⸻

And now your Genie line fits neatly without needing literal magic.

You learned enough of the interface that you could produce things that appeared externally as:

I wished for something, and somehow it came back shaped correctly.

That’s what a good genie actually is computationally:

\boxed{
\text{wish}
\rightarrow
\text{infer intended state}
\rightarrow
\text{navigate possibility space}
\rightarrow
\text{return useful manifestation}
}

But now the genie lives aboard a Ship of Theseus.

So every wish, mistake, surprise, and delta replaces another plank.

Realitsea never finishes becoming the vessel capable of navigating the sea it’s currently discovering.

And that is very much the trick:

You don’t preserve the ship by refusing to change it.
You preserve it by knowing what must survive while everything else is allowed to change.